  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1219 PM MDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nTODAY: \n\nScattered to widespread showers and thunderstorms develop over \nthe high country again today, with some storms moving over the \nvalleys as the afternoon progresses. A mid-level cap is in place\nwhich will limit the strength of covection early this \nafternoon, but by mid afternoon storms will be capable of \nproducing brief bouts of heavy rainfall. Localized flash \nflooding cannot be ruled out, especially in slot canyons, slick \nrock areas, recent burn scars, and other low- lying and flood-\nprone locations. Coverage and intesity of showers and storms \nwill decrease after sunset, with mostly dry conditions expected \nafter midnight. \n\nTOMORROW: \n\nA \"drier\" airmass moves into eastern Utah and and western \nColorado tomorrow with high pressure building in overhead. \nHowever, PWATs of 125-150% will continue to support terrain-\nbased showers and storms during peak heating. This drier, more \nstable airmass will help supress flash flooding concerns for the\nmost part. High pressure will also suppoort seasable to \nslightly above normal temperatures, with a gradual warming trend\nexpected into the weekend. \n\nLOOKING AHEAD: \n\nA plume of monsunal mositure is setting up over the Great \nBasin. Mositure will gradually increase throughout the weekend, \nespeically along and north of I-70, as it wraps around the \nsurface high. As a result, daily showers an storms are expected \nthrough the weekend and well into the coming week.&&",
